# gterm custom commands — the GTC guide

Every command in this terminal is a `.json` file. This directory (`/system/gterm/`)
IS the command set: the terminal reads it at startup, `help` lists what it finds,
Tab-completion completes what it finds, and you can add your own commands just by
dropping a new `.json` file here (run `reload`, or just type the command — unknown
commands trigger a rescan automatically).

This guide teaches the format. You can read it in the OS itself:

    cat /system/gterm/guide.md

...and every shipped command file is a working example — open a few:

    cat /system/gterm/uptime.json
    cat /system/gterm/kill.json


## 1. The idea in one screen

A command file is a JSON object with metadata plus a list of steps. Steps can
perform real kernel syscalls — the same `int 0x80` calls the C built-ins use —
so a JSON command is genuine OS code, not a macro.

Minimal custom command (`hello.json`):

```json
{
  "name": "hello",
  "category": "custom",
  "summary": "say hello",
  "usage": "hello [name]",
  "args": [ { "name": "name" } ],
  "steps": [
    { "if": "$# >= 1",
      "then": [ { "print": "hello, $1!" } ],
      "else": [ { "print": "hello, world" } ] }
  ]
}
```

Drop that file in `/system/gterm/` (or `/home/user/.gterm/` — scanned second, so
your copy wins on name clashes), type `hello SwirlOS`, and it runs. `help` lists
it under `custom`, Tab completes it.


## 2. Where commands come from

| directory | role |
|------------|------|
| `/system/gterm/` | the shipped command set + your additions |
| `/home/user/.gterm/` | personal commands; same name here overrides the system one |

- Files must end in `.json`; everything else in the directory is ignored
  (`guide.md` lives here too, harmlessly).
- One command per file is the convention: the file `ls.json` defines `ls`.
  The `"name"` field is what actually counts — the file name is only a fallback.
- Commands are looked up case-insensitively; names are lowercased at load.
- The `reload` command rescans both directories at any time.
- A command not found also triggers one rescan before giving up — so a file you
  just created usually works immediately.


## 3. Metadata fields

```json
{
  "name":     "mycmd",                  // required (or file name is used)
  "aliases":  ["mc", "my"],            // extra names, max 4
  "category": "custom",                // files | system | network | session | custom
  "summary": "one line for 'help'",    // shown right of the usage column
  "usage":    "mycmd <thing>",         // shown in 'help'; defaults to the name
  "args":     [ { "name": "thing" } ], // names for $1.. (optional, cosmetic)
  "engine":   "pipeline",              // "pipeline" = run the steps below
                                        // "builtin"  = C code in the terminal
  "steps":    [ ... ]
}
```

- `"engine"`: if you omit it, a file with a `steps` array is a pipeline, anything
  else is a builtin — but write it out; it documents intent.
- `"args"` names your positional arguments: with the example above, `$thing`
  and `$1` are the same, which reads nicer in steps.
- `//` line comments are allowed anywhere (the parser skips them). Trailing
  commas are tolerated too.

**Builtins.** A handful of commands (the nano editor, the ping engine, DOOM...)
are still C code inside the terminal. Their files carry only metadata — but
they are still the override hook: if you write your own `ls.json` with
`"engine": "pipeline"`, *yours* runs instead of the C one. The JSON always wins.


## 4. Steps

Each step is one object with exactly one action key. They run top to bottom.

### print — output
```json
{ "print": "uptime is $si.uptime_cs cs", "color": "ok", "newline": true }
```
Colors: `out` (default), `ok`, `err`, `accent`, `prompt`. `"newline": false`
keeps the cursor on the same line.

### print_raw — exact bytes from the @buf scratch buffer
```json
{ "print_raw": "@buf", "len": "$n" }
```
No substitution, no auto-newline: this is how file reads are echoed byte-exact.

### var — set a variable
```json
{ "var": "p",   "value": "text with $1 inside" }
{ "var": "kb",  "expr": "($si.mem_used + 1023) / 1024" }
{ "var": "p",   "path": "notes/todo.txt" }
```
`"value"` copies text after substitution; `"expr"` evaluates integer math
(see §6); `"path"` resolves a relative path against the working directory
(the same rule the shell uses for its own file commands).

### syscall — call the kernel
```json
{ "syscall": "open", "args": ["$p", 1], "as": "fd" }
```
`"args"` matches the syscall's argument list (see §7). `"as"` stores the return
value under a name; when the syscall fills a struct, `"as"` binds the struct
fields instead and the return lands in `$RET`.

### setbuf — fill the @buf scratch buffer
```json
{ "setbuf": "GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n", "as": "n" }
```
Loads text (after substitution) into the 2 KB scratch buffer and stores its
length. `"as"` is optional. Pair with `write`/`sock_send`.

### if — branch
```json
{ "if": "$rc == 0", "then": [ ...steps... ], "else": [ ...steps... ] }
```
Both arrays are optional. Nest `if`s inside `else` for multi-way chains (see
`kill.json`).

### loop — repeat
```json
{ "loop": { "until": "$RET == 0", "max": 1024 }, "steps": [ ... ] }
```
Other forms: `"while": "$n > 0"`, `"times": 5`. `max` (default 256, hard cap
4096) protects you from infinite loops. Conditions are checked before each
iteration.

### spawn — start a program
```json
{ "spawn": "/system/apps/gui/gsettings.elf" }
{ "spawn": "/system/apps/winrun.elf", "arg": "$1", "wait": true, "as": "code" }
```
`"arg"` uses the spawn-with-argument call, `"wait"` blocks until it exits and
`"as"` gets the exit code. On failure a message is printed for you.

`"follow": "<file>"` (with `spawn`) is for text programs: the file is emptied,
the program is started, and the terminal streams whatever the program appends
to that file until it exits (Ctrl+C kills it). Lines starting with the marker
bytes 0x02/0x03/0x04 are shown green/red/accent. This is how `spm` prints.
```json
{ "spawn": "/system/apps/spm.elf", "arg": "$@", "follow": "/home/user/.spm/out.log" }
```

### action — touch shell state
```json
{ "action": "cd", "path": "/home/user" }
{ "action": "clear" }
{ "action": "close" }
```
`cd` runs the shell's own cd (validation, `..` cleanup, tab title updates);
`clear` wipes the scrollback; `close` closes the terminal window (same as `exit`).

### exit — stop the command
```json
{ "exit": 0 }
```
Anything after it is skipped. (The number is documentation only.)


## 5. Substitution — the `$` language

Inside any string value (print text, paths, args, conditions):

| reference | meaning |
|-----------|---------|
| `$0` … `$9` | argv slots; `$0` is the command name itself |
| `$name` | a named arg from `"args"` or a variable |
| `$#` | how many arguments the user typed |
| `$@` | all arguments joined with spaces |
| `$CWD` | the working directory |
| `$RET` | the last syscall's return value |
| `$$` | a literal `$` |
| `${name:02}` | zero-pad an integer to 2 digits (any width) |

Unknown references are printed **verbatim** (`$typo` stays `$typo`), so a
mistake is visible instead of silently empty.

Struct fields come from the syscall that produced them (§7):

```json
{ "syscall": "sysinfo", "as": "si" },     // $si.hostname, $si.mem_total, ...
{ "syscall": "rtc6",    "as": "now" },    // $now.y $now.mo $now.d $now.h $now.mi $now.s
{ "syscall": "dir_read", "args": ["$h"], "as": "e" },   // $e.name, $e.type
{ "syscall": "proc_list", "args": ["@pl", 16], "as": "pl" }
//   $pl            -> the record count
//   $pl[2].pid     -> 3rd process: .pid .ppid .state .name
```


## 6. Expressions and conditions

`"expr"` supports integer math on `$refs` and literals:
`+ - * / %`, parentheses, unary minus, decimal and `0x` hex. Division by zero
evaluates to 0. Example: `"$si.uptime_cs / 360000"`.

Conditions are `"left OP right"` with spaces (both sides get substituted
first — quote-like operands don't need quotes, just avoid spaces):

| OP | compares |
|----|----------|
| `==` `!=` | numbers if both sides are numeric, else strings |
| `<` `<=` `>` `>=` | numbers, else alphabetical |
| `starts` | left begins with right (`"$1 starts /"` = absolute path?) |
| `contains` | left contains right anywhere |

`"$1 starts -"` is how you detect flags.


## 7. The syscall table

These are the calls a command file may make (kernel `int 0x80`, ABI numbers in
brackets; the full ABI is documented in `/system/doc/syscall-abi.txt`).
Arg letters: **s** = string, **i** = integer, **B** = the @buf scratch buffer,
**E/S/T/P** = the kernel fills a struct for you.

| syscall | args | returns |
|---------|------|---------|
| `open` [0] | path, flags (1=READ 2=WRITE 4=CREATE 8=TRUNC) | fd |
| `read` [1] | fd, **B**, max | bytes read (0 = EOF) |
| `write` [2] | fd, **B**, len | bytes written |
| `close` [3] | fd | 0 |
| `seek` [4] | fd, off, whence (0=set 1=cur 2=end) | new offset |
| `dir_open` [5] | path | handle |
| `dir_read` [6] | handle, **E** | 1 = entry in `$e`, 0 = end |
| `spawn` [7] | path | pid |
| `wait` [8] | pid | exit code |
| `getpid` [10] / `ppid` [11] / `getring` [20] / `yield` [30] | | id / ring |
| `sysinfo` [12] | **S** | fills `$si.*` |
| `mkdir` [17] | path | 0 |
| `unlink` [18] | path | 0 (-3 = not empty) |
| `proc_list` [19] | **P**, max | record count |
| `kill` [28] | pid | 0 / -2 protected / -3 self |
| `rename` [29] | old, new | 0 |
| `rtc` [31] | | packed 0x00HHMMSS |
| `unlink_rec` [32] | path | 0 (recursive delete!) |
| `rtc6` [35] | **T** | fills `$now.*` |
| `power` [39] | 0=off 1=reboot | -1 if the machine refused |
| `wait_nb` [46] | pid | reaped pid or 0 |
| `clip_get` [48] | **B**, max | clipboard length |
| `sleep` [49] | ms | 0 (a real park, not a spin) |
| `spawn_a` [50] | path, arg | pid |
| `notify_send` [58] | title, body, kind 0..3 | 0 (toast popup) |
| `beep` [60] | freq Hz, ms | 0 |
| `setvol` [61] | 0..100 | 0 |
| `net_ping_start` [67] | ip, count | 0 started |
| `sock_open` [70] | 1=TCP 2=UDP | handle |
| `sock_connect` [71] | h, ip, port | 0 |
| `sock_send` [72] | h, **B**, len | bytes |
| `sock_recv` [73] | h, **B**, max | bytes (0 = none) |
| `sock_close` [74] / `sock_poll` [75] | h | |
| `kbd_mods` [78] | | shift=1 ctrl=2 alt=4 |

`$si.*` fields: `uptime_cs`, `mem_total`, `mem_used`, `nprocs`, `font_size`,
`cpu_pct`, `hostname`. Error codes overall: -1 bad args, -2 not found,
-3 no access, -4 exists, -5 no resources.

Window-manager and other dangerous calls are deliberately NOT reachable from
command files — a broken JSON cannot corrupt this terminal's own window.


## 8. Redirection

`command > file` is handled by the engine for pipeline commands: every `print`
and `print_raw` of that run lands in the file instead (opened WRITE|CREATE|TRUNC
against the working directory), and a `wrote <path> (N bytes)` line confirms it.

    echo shopping list > notes.txt      # classic usage
    cat /system/gterm/guide.md > /home/user/guide-copy.txt


## 9. Recipes

### beep — one-liner
```json
{ "name": "beep", "category": "custom", "summary": "make some noise",
  "usage": "beep [hz] [ms]", "args": [ { "name": "hz" }, { "name": "ms" } ],
  "engine": "pipeline", "steps": [
    { "if": "$# < 1",
      "then": [ { "syscall": "beep", "args": [880, 120] } ],
      "else": [ { "syscall": "beep", "args": ["$hz", "$ms"] } ] }
  ] }
```

### note — quick file append demo
```json
{ "name": "note", "category": "custom", "summary": "write a one-line note",
  "usage": "note <text>", "engine": "pipeline", "steps": [
    { "if": "$# < 1",
      "then": [ { "print": "usage: note <text>", "color": "err" } ],
      "else": [ { "print": "$@", "newline": true } ] }
  ] }
```
Use it as `note buy coffee > notes.txt` — the engine writes the file.

### dircount — loop over a directory
```json
{ "name": "dircount", "category": "custom", "summary": "count entries in a dir",
  "usage": "dircount [path]", "args": [ { "name": "path" } ],
  "engine": "pipeline", "steps": [
    { "var": "p", "path": "$path" },
    { "syscall": "dir_open", "args": ["$p"], "as": "h" },
    { "if": "$h < 0",
      "then": [ { "print": "dircount: cannot open $p", "color": "err" },
                { "exit": 0 } ] },
    { "var": "n", "value": "0" },
    { "loop": { "until": "$RET == 0", "max": 512 }, "steps": [
        { "syscall": "dir_read", "args": ["$h"], "as": "e" },
        { "if": "$RET > 0",
          "then": [ { "var": "n", "expr": "$n + 1" } ] }
    ] },
    { "syscall": "close", "args": ["$h"] },
    { "print": "$p: $n entries", "color": "ok" }
  ] }
```

### who — process list from JSON
```json
{ "name": "who", "category": "custom", "summary": "pid + name of every process",
  "usage": "who", "engine": "pipeline", "steps": [
    { "syscall": "proc_list", "args": ["@pl", 16], "as": "pl" },
    { "var": "i", "value": "0" },
    { "loop": { "while": "$i < $pl" }, "steps": [
        { "print": "  $pl[$i].pid  $pl[$i].name" },
        { "var": "i", "expr": "$i + 1" }
    ] }
  ] }
```
`$pl` is the record count, `$pl[i].pid` / `.ppid` / `.state` / `.name` the fields.

### alarm — notify + beep
```json
{ "name": "alarm", "category": "custom", "summary": "beep and pop a toast",
  "usage": "alarm [seconds]", "args": [ { "name": "seconds" } ],
  "engine": "pipeline", "steps": [
    { "var": "s", "value": "1" },
    { "if": "$# >= 1", "then": [ { "var": "s", "value": "$seconds" } ] },
    { "var": "ms", "expr": "$s * 1000" },
    { "syscall": "sleep", "args": ["$ms"] },
    { "syscall": "notify_send", "args": ["alarm", "time is up", 2] },
    { "syscall": "beep", "args": [880, 400] }
  ] }
```


## 10. Limits & troubleshooting

- One step can print at most ~500 chars; variables hold up to ~190.
- A command may run at most 20000 steps and loop at most 4096 times per loop
  (the interpreter aborts with a visible error instead of hanging the terminal).
- Files are capped at 8 KB each, 64 commands total, nesting depth 8.
- A broken file is skipped with a one-line error (shown when it loads or when
  you run `reload`); the rest keep working. A crashed command at worst takes
  down its own terminal window — the OS keeps running, that is the point of
  gterm being its own process.
- If your command does nothing, check for a `$var` that never got set: unset
  references print verbatim, so a literal `$fd` in the output is the tell.

The format is parsed by `user/gterm_cmds.c` in the OS source tree — when in
doubt, that file is the truth. Have fun.
